{"items":[{"tags":["api","openfda"],"owner":{"account_id":47195274,"reputation":1,"user_id":42927,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e13561ffb983b08b7e481f9b2502c7c7?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Justin Stout","link":"https://opendata.stackexchange.com/users/42927/justin-stout"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":10,"answer_count":0,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1791553984,"creation_date":1791553984,"question_id":23217,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://opendata.stackexchange.com/questions/23217/fda-orange-book-api-returns-different-strength-values-than-legacy-orange-book-te","title":"FDA Orange Book API returns different strength values than legacy Orange Book text files for kits/copackaged products and base-equivalent strengths","body":"<p>I'm evaluating the newly hosted FDA Orange Book API and have noticed some differences in the strength data compared with the legacy Orange Book downloadable text files and the Orange Book web UI.</p>
<p>I'm trying to understand whether these differences are expected transformations, API limitations, or potential data issues.</p>
<p>Example 1: Copackaged / Kit Products</p>
<p>For application N220641 (Lifyorli Copackaged), the Orange Book web search displays a strength of:</p>
<p>100MG;25MG</p>
<p>However, the API response returns only:</p>
<p>{
&quot;active_ingredients&quot;: [
{
&quot;name&quot;: &quot;RELACORILANT&quot;,
&quot;strength&quot;: &quot;100MG&quot;
}
]
}</p>
<p>The second strength component (25MG) is not present in the API output.</p>
<p>Questions:</p>
<p>For copackaged products or kits, is the API intended to return only the strength associated with the listed active ingredient?</p>
<p>Should all package component strengths be represented in the API, similar to the web UI and historical text files?</p>
<p>Is there any documentation describing how kit/copackaged strengths are mapped into the new API schema?</p>
<p>Example 2: Base vs Salt Equivalent Strengths</p>
<p>I'm also seeing differences for products where the legacy Orange Book files historically expressed strength using both a salt form and a base-equivalent expression.</p>
<p>In some cases, the API appears to return only one representation of the strength, whereas the historical files contained additional strength information or equivalent-base values.</p>
<p>For application N020971 (SEPTOCAINE), the Orange Book web search displays a strength of:</p>
<p>4%; EQ 0.0085MG BASE/1.7ML (4%; EQ 0.005MG BASE/ML)
and
4%; EQ 0.017MG BASE/1.7ML (4%; EQ 0.01MG BASE/ML)</p>
<p>However, the API response returns only:</p>
<p>{
&quot;active_ingredients&quot;: [
{
&quot;name&quot;: &quot;ARTICAINE HYDROCHLORIDE&quot;,
&quot;strength&quot;: &quot;4%&quot;
},
{
&quot;name&quot;: &quot;EPINEPHRINE BITARTRATE&quot;,
&quot;strength&quot;: &quot;EQ 0.017MG BASE/1.7ML (4%&quot;
}
],</p>
<p>[
{
&quot;active_ingredients&quot;: [
{
&quot;name&quot;: &quot;ARTICAINE HYDROCHLORIDE&quot;,
&quot;strength&quot;: &quot;4%&quot;
},
{
&quot;name&quot;: &quot;EPINEPHRINE BITARTRATE&quot;,
&quot;strength&quot;: &quot;EQ 0.0085MG BASE/1.7ML (4%&quot;
}
]</p>
<p>Questions:</p>
<p>Has the Orange Book API intentionally standardized strength representation for salt/base-equivalent products?</p>
<p>Is there a documented rule for determining when the API returns:</p>
<p>salt strength,
base-equivalent strength,
or both?</p>
<p>Are the API values expected to be semantically equivalent to the legacy downloadable Orange Book files, or should consumers expect data normalization differences?</p>
<p>Research Performed</p>
<p>Reviewed the Orange Book web interface output.</p>
<p>Compared API JSON responses against historical Orange Book text-file extracts.</p>
<p>Observed discrepancies for both copackaged products/kits and certain base-equivalent strength presentations.</p>
<p>Example API endpoint:</p>
<p><a href="https://api.fda.gov/drug/orangebook.json?search=products.application_number:220641" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://api.fda.gov/drug/orangebook.json?search=products.application_number:220641</a></p>
<p>Has anyone from FDA or other Orange Book consumers identified similar differences, or is there documentation describing how the new API derives the strength field relative to the legacy Orange Book files?</p>
